# 7.B Decorative Image

Test Requirement
Recall that some decorative images are pure decoration, used only for visual formatting or are not presented visually to users. These images should always be marked as decorative to hide them from assistive technology (AT). However, for other images, it is not always clear whether they should be marked as decorative or meaningful. Web page authors may use images illustrate text content, suggest mood or tone, or create specific sensory experiences.

For example, a paragraph about dogs might be accompanied by an image of a dog. It might seem obvious to consider this image as decorative. However, the author could have chosen the image to show a specific example or to add additional information, such as “My dog looks so happy.”

