As such, this test starts with the assumption that images with empty accessible names and images that are skipped by ANDI were intended by the author to be decorative. However, we still need to confirm that these images do not serve any important function, such as conveying information not available elsewhere on the page.

There are various ways to mark up an image as decorative, such as:

alt = “”
role= “presentation”
role= “none”
aria-hidden= “true”
Inserting it as a CSS background image
These will result in the image having an empty accessible name or description and being ignored by AT. However, this is different from an image that is missing accessible name markup altogether. Since this missing markup could result in confusing behavior from AT, such as reading the image’s path and filename, we will also be checking for this error in this test. Background images will be tested in 7.C Background Image.

The results from this test are used to determine if WCAG SC 1.1.1 Non-Text Content is met.

## Method
Visual and manual inspection of the page

### Tool
- ANDI: Graphics/Images

## Identifying Content
If available, select the “find background” button in ANDI: graphics/images to highlight all background images. (This button is not available if no background images are detected.) Exclude them from this test and test them under 7.C. Screenshot to illustrate preceding descriptions.
Use ANDI: graphics/images to find all images on the page with empty accessible names (blank ANDI Output). Screenshot to illustrate preceding descriptions.
Use ANDI: Focusable Elements (and/or ANDI: Links/Buttons if applicable) to check the ANDI Output of keyboard-focusable images, as they may be components of focusable elements. Use these results if they are different from the output of ANDI: Graphics/Images.
For example, an image with no accessible name markup might normally be flagged by ANDI as having no accessible name. However, if the image is a component or child of a link, it might include the link’s innertext as its ANDI Output. In the example below, a link with the text of “Tulips” contains an image without accessible name markup. Both ANDI: Graphics/Images and ANDI: Focusable Elements shows that the ANDI Output is also “Tulips.” Screenshot to illustrate preceding descriptions.

Use ANDI: graphics/images to find all images on the page with no accessible names (“The image has no accessible name, [alt], or [title]”).
Screenshot to illustrate preceding descriptions.
Use visual inspection along with ANDI to determine if there are images skipped (not navigated to) by ANDI.
Because ANDI might not highlight or provide information for some images that have been marked up to be hidden from AT, for example, images with role=“presentation”, you will have to use a combination of visual inspection and ANDI’s Next Element button to determine if there are images that are skipped by ANDI. They should be included in this test.
Note:
ANDI may skip background images and images with role=“presentation”, role=“none”, or aria-hidden=“true“.

## Does Not Apply
This test DOES NOT APPLY (DNA) to background images, images with non-empty accessible names, or if there are no images on the page.
